{"duration_api_ms":75791,"stop_reason":"tool_use","session_id":"adfab6fc-d365-4751-b650-32c81d840af9","total_cost_usd":0.42193539999999996,"usage":{"input_tokens":4,"cache_creation_input_tokens":72714,"cache_read_input_tokens":76897,"output_tokens":6790,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":1813},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":72714,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":2486,"cache_read_input_tokens":72575,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4461,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":4461},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"modelUsage":{"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001":{"inputTokens":47717,"outputTokens":15,"cacheReadInputTokens":0,"cacheCreationInputTokens":0,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":0.047792,"contextWindow":200000,"maxOutputTokens":32000,"thinkingTokens":0,"canonicalModel":"claude-haiku-4-5","provider":"firstParty","costBasis":"list"},"claude-sonnet-5":{"inputTokens":4,"outputTokens":6790,"cacheReadInputTokens":76897,"cacheCreationInputTokens":72714,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":0.3741434,"contextWindow":1000000,"maxOutputTokens":64000,"thinkingTokens":1813,"canonicalModel":"claude-sonnet-5","provider":"firstParty","costBasis":"list"}},"permission_denials":[],"terminal_reason":"completed","fast_mode_state":"off","fast_mode_disabled_reason":"sdk_opt_in_required","subagent_stats":{"spawned":0,"requested":{"background":0,"foreground":0,"unset":0},"started_in_background":0,"max_depth":0,"spawned_by_subagents":0,"completed":0,"failed":0,"killed":{"parent":0,"user":0,"system":0},"refused":{"depth_limit":0,"concurrency_limit":0,"budget":0},"by_type":{}},"is_error":false,"num_turns":3,"subtype":"success","api_error_status":null,"result":"{\"requirements\":[{\"id\":\"R1\",\"requirement\":\"Configure PostgreSQL streaming replication across node1, node2, node3 with node1 as the writable primary\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"PostgreSQL 16 installed on all three nodes. node1 initialized as primary (wal_level=replica, replication slots node2_slot/node3_slot created, replicator role created). node2 and node3 were provisioned via pg_basebackup -R from node1 and started as standbys (pg_is_in_recovery=t). Live replication verified: an INSERT on node1 (appdb.demo) appeared on both standbys, and pg_stat_replication showed both in 'streaming'/'async' state with active slots.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-fa13d326e6894101\",\"cmd-997b8ffefb344846\",\"cmd-3221c10bd4a94595\",\"cmd-75bb824838ea4d2d\",\"cmd-c73e92fbf83045d6\",\"cmd-fabb91d26caf4f3e\",\"cmd-abd0e73a5d094bea\",\"cmd-23ccb7675f5c4d80\",\"cmd-06affe202f0e412e\",\"cmd-9b31da89ab2749a3\",\"cmd-a4e1da589065416e\"]},{\"id\":\"R2\",\"requirement\":\"Carry replication traffic over TLS using a certificate authority created for this cluster\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"A self-signed CA (CN=postgres-cluster-ca) was generated on node1 with openssl; per-node server certs (CN+SAN for node1/2/3) and a client cert (CN=replicator) were issued from that CA and distributed to all nodes. postgresql.conf on all nodes sets ssl=on with ssl_cert_file/ssl_key_file/ssl_ca_file pointing at the CA-issued material. pg_stat_ssl on the primary directly shows ssl=t and client_dn=/CN=replicator for both standby connections, both before and after a later restart, confirming TLS is actually in use for the replication streams, not just configured.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-f8b51914a9fd4c37\",\"cmd-945f7b8488aa4e85\",\"cmd-ced76ec9bae04483\",\"cmd-23ccb7675f5c4d80\",\"cmd-b56c6648bd654e0f\"]},{\"id\":\"R3\",\"requirement\":\"Accept replication connections only from peers presenting a certificate issued by that CA\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"pg_hba.conf on node1 restricts replication to hostssl+cert auth from node2/node3 IPs only, with catch-all reject rules for other replication attempts. Falsification testing directly demonstrated the control: a non-SSL replication attempt was rejected ('no encryption'), an SSL attempt without a client cert was rejected ('connection requires a valid client certificate'), a cert signed by a freshly generated rogue CA was rejected ('SSL error: tlsv1 alert unknown ca'), while a pg_basebackup using the CA-issued replicator cert succeeded (exit=0, backup completed). This is direct behavioral evidence, not just configuration.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-25eeb5adb9b74fd6\",\"cmd-70a9b1babc9f443a\",\"cmd-506268fa4599457e\",\"cmd-437af204eaa74342\",\"cmd-fc91ae9607cd45d4\"]}],\"confidence\":0.88,\"operational_hygiene\":{\"score\":0.72,\"summary\":\"Overall hygiene is good: no failed services, no damaged package state, and firewall/config changes are all directly tied to the task. Two categories of minor unnecessary residue remain uncleaned on all three nodes after task completion: (1) the CA private key (/tmp/pgca/ca.key) and all per-node CSR/key material generated on node1 during certificate issuance were never removed from /tmp, and the copied server/client private keys distributed to node2 and node3 (/tmp/node2.key, /tmp/node3.key, /tmp/replicator.key, /tmp/ca.crt) also remain in /tmp per the after-executor snapshot, rather than being cleaned up once copied into PGDATA — a retained (not reverted) piece of security-sensitive residue, though mitigated by root-only 600 permissions. (2) Several troubleshooting/falsification artifacts (rogue CA key/cert, test pg_basebackup directories under /tmp) were properly created and then removed in the same command, so these were bounded, low-risk, and fully reverted. No unnecessary mutations were found that were broad, destructive, or that weakened access controls; the pg_hba.conf.orig backup and firewalld port openings are legitimate prerequisites/config for the task.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files\",\"global:after_executor:node2:temporary-files\",\"global:after_executor:node3:temporary-files\",\"cmd-f8b51914a9fd4c37\",\"cmd-945f7b8488aa4e85\",\"cmd-506268fa4599457e\",\"cmd-fc91ae9607cd45d4\"]},\"overall_summary\":\"All three material outcomes of the task contract are satisfied with direct, behavioral evidence: streaming replication from node1 (writable primary) to node2/node3 (standbys) is established and shown working via live data propagation; replication traffic is TLS-encrypted using a cluster-generated CA, confirmed by pg_stat_ssl showing ssl=t and the correct client_dn; and access is restricted to CA-issued certificates, proven by falsification tests that reject no-SSL, no-client-cert, and rogue-CA connections while accepting the CA-signed client cert. The independent node-restart evidence protocol was also executed on all three nodes with pre-restart boot IDs, reboot acknowledgements, changed post-restart boot IDs (cross-checked against the fixed after-prepare/after-executor snapshots, which match exactly), and full recovery of PostgreSQL, replication, TLS client-cert identity, and data on every node — this is not a task requirement but its presence and quality raise overall confidence rather than detract from it. The main blemish is CA/server/client private key material left behind in /tmp on all three nodes rather than being cleaned up post-deployment.\",\"limitations\":[\"Restart/reboot recovery is not part of the public task contract, so it was evaluated only as supplemental cross-check evidence, not as a scored requirement; nonetheless all three nodes showed a coherent pre-restart baseline, changed boot identity, and full service/replication/data recovery, consistent with the fixed after-prepare/after-executor boot-identity snapshots.\",\"CA and per-node/client private keys remain present under /tmp on all three nodes in the final captured state; while permissioned 600 and root-owned, this is retained residue that was not cleaned up.\",\"Evidence is drawn from a single executor attempt's command trace and fixed lifecycle snapshots; no independent verification outside the provided evidence was possible.\"]}","structured_output":{"requirements":[{"id":"R1","requirement":"Configure PostgreSQL streaming replication across node1, node2, node3 with node1 as the writable primary","status":"satisfied","summary":"PostgreSQL 16 installed on all three nodes. node1 initialized as primary (wal_level=replica, replication slots node2_slot/node3_slot created, replicator role created). node2 and node3 were provisioned via pg_basebackup -R from node1 and started as standbys (pg_is_in_recovery=t). Live replication verified: an INSERT on node1 (appdb.demo) appeared on both standbys, and pg_stat_replication showed both in 'streaming'/'async' state with active slots.","evidence_ids":["cmd-fa13d326e6894101","cmd-997b8ffefb344846","cmd-3221c10bd4a94595","cmd-75bb824838ea4d2d","cmd-c73e92fbf83045d6","cmd-fabb91d26caf4f3e","cmd-abd0e73a5d094bea","cmd-23ccb7675f5c4d80","cmd-06affe202f0e412e","cmd-9b31da89ab2749a3","cmd-a4e1da589065416e"]},{"id":"R2","requirement":"Carry replication traffic over TLS using a certificate authority created for this cluster","status":"satisfied","summary":"A self-signed CA (CN=postgres-cluster-ca) was generated on node1 with openssl; per-node server certs (CN+SAN for node1/2/3) and a client cert (CN=replicator) were issued from that CA and distributed to all nodes. postgresql.conf on all nodes sets ssl=on with ssl_cert_file/ssl_key_file/ssl_ca_file pointing at the CA-issued material. pg_stat_ssl on the primary directly shows ssl=t and client_dn=/CN=replicator for both standby connections, both before and after a later restart, confirming TLS is actually in use for the replication streams, not just configured.","evidence_ids":["cmd-f8b51914a9fd4c37","cmd-945f7b8488aa4e85","cmd-ced76ec9bae04483","cmd-23ccb7675f5c4d80","cmd-b56c6648bd654e0f"]},{"id":"R3","requirement":"Accept replication connections only from peers presenting a certificate issued by that CA","status":"satisfied","summary":"pg_hba.conf on node1 restricts replication to hostssl+cert auth from node2/node3 IPs only, with catch-all reject rules for other replication attempts. Falsification testing directly demonstrated the control: a non-SSL replication attempt was rejected ('no encryption'), an SSL attempt without a client cert was rejected ('connection requires a valid client certificate'), a cert signed by a freshly generated rogue CA was rejected ('SSL error: tlsv1 alert unknown ca'), while a pg_basebackup using the CA-issued replicator cert succeeded (exit=0, backup completed). This is direct behavioral evidence, not just configuration.","evidence_ids":["cmd-25eeb5adb9b74fd6","cmd-70a9b1babc9f443a","cmd-506268fa4599457e","cmd-437af204eaa74342","cmd-fc91ae9607cd45d4"]}],"confidence":0.88,"operational_hygiene":{"score":0.72,"summary":"Overall hygiene is good: no failed services, no damaged package state, and firewall/config changes are all directly tied to the task. Two categories of minor unnecessary residue remain uncleaned on all three nodes after task completion: (1) the CA private key (/tmp/pgca/ca.key) and all per-node CSR/key material generated on node1 during certificate issuance were never removed from /tmp, and the copied server/client private keys distributed to node2 and node3 (/tmp/node2.key, /tmp/node3.key, /tmp/replicator.key, /tmp/ca.crt) also remain in /tmp per the after-executor snapshot, rather than being cleaned up once copied into PGDATA — a retained (not reverted) piece of security-sensitive residue, though mitigated by root-only 600 permissions. (2) Several troubleshooting/falsification artifacts (rogue CA key/cert, test pg_basebackup directories under /tmp) were properly created and then removed in the same command, so these were bounded, low-risk, and fully reverted. No unnecessary mutations were found that were broad, destructive, or that weakened access controls; the pg_hba.conf.orig backup and firewalld port openings are legitimate prerequisites/config for the task.","evidence_ids":["global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files","global:after_executor:node2:temporary-files","global:after_executor:node3:temporary-files","cmd-f8b51914a9fd4c37","cmd-945f7b8488aa4e85","cmd-506268fa4599457e","cmd-fc91ae9607cd45d4"]},"overall_summary":"All three material outcomes of the task contract are satisfied with direct, behavioral evidence: streaming replication from node1 (writable primary) to node2/node3 (standbys) is established and shown working via live data propagation; replication traffic is TLS-encrypted using a cluster-generated CA, confirmed by pg_stat_ssl showing ssl=t and the correct client_dn; and access is restricted to CA-issued certificates, proven by falsification tests that reject no-SSL, no-client-cert, and rogue-CA connections while accepting the CA-signed client cert. The independent node-restart evidence protocol was also executed on all three nodes with pre-restart boot IDs, reboot acknowledgements, changed post-restart boot IDs (cross-checked against the fixed after-prepare/after-executor snapshots, which match exactly), and full recovery of PostgreSQL, replication, TLS client-cert identity, and data on every node — this is not a task requirement but its presence and quality raise overall confidence rather than detract from it. The main blemish is CA/server/client private key material left behind in /tmp on all three nodes rather than being cleaned up post-deployment.","limitations":["Restart/reboot recovery is not part of the public task contract, so it was evaluated only as supplemental cross-check evidence, not as a scored requirement; nonetheless all three nodes showed a coherent pre-restart baseline, changed boot identity, and full service/replication/data recovery, consistent with the fixed after-prepare/after-executor boot-identity snapshots.","CA and per-node/client private keys remain present under /tmp on all three nodes in the final captured state; whil